current affordance network but not the alternatives that were pruned, so as the network collapses, the organization loses the conceptual scaffolding needed to navigate out of the design basin. Every new change deepens the basin by reinforcing the only design path anyone can still reason about.", "probability": 0.054}, {"conjecture": "Each well-tested change to an interface eliminates nearby alternative interfaces by making them redundant with the optimized one, so over time the affordance network collapses to a single path. The system becomes stranded because any change to the surviving interface now requires rebuilding the entire eliminated affordance network from scratch.", "probability": 0.063}, {"conjective": "Repeated optimization of an interface causes its consumers to shed their own fallback affordances, collapsing the broader affordance network into a deep basin of mutual dependency. Changing any single interface becomes infeasible because all nearby alternatives were eliminated not in the interface itself but in every system that learned to rely on its optimized form.", "probability": 0.057}, {"conjecture": "The act of optimizing an interface's affordances creates a gravity well that attracts future changes to extend rather than replace it, collapsing the affordance network through cumulative path dependence. The design basin deepens with each extension until the nearest viable alternative design is farther than any incremental change can reach.", "probability": 0.066}, {"conjecture": "Interfaces that are repeatedly optimized acquire affordances for edge cases that were never anticipated by nearby alternative designs, collapsing the network by making alternatives appear incomplete. The system becomes stranded in a deep basin because any replacement interface must simultaneously reproduce all the emergent affordances or face rejection during review.", "probability": 0.059}]}
